@prefix governed: <http://example.org/orexis/firmware/moisture-sensor#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/firmware/moisture-sensor#> .
@prefix sensing: <http://example.org/orexis/sensing#> .
@prefix ssn:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix mc:   <http://example.org/orexis/microcontroller#> .
@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix device: <http://example.org/orexis/device#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/firmware/moisture-sensor> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "The governed node — what a board running this firmware IS" ;
    rdfs:comment """A firmware describes itself (#175), so a world does not have to. This is
a-part-is-described-once-and-fitted-many-times applied to code instead of silicon: which
procedures a board performs is decided the moment it is flashed — the derivation rules' own
comment has always said 'reflash a board from push to scheduled and the agent gains an
interval' — so those facts belong to the firmware, stated once, and a board just says which
one it runs. A world types its connecting device `governed:Node` and the sense mode and the
firmware name follow by the closure's hasValue rule; restating them per board becomes
needless, and contradicting them becomes refusable.

What stays the WORLD's is the deployment half: which subject, which topics, which pins, which
calibration, which bus — the same boundary deploy-dht22.ttl draws for parts. The alarm promise
moved HERE from the world (#181): this firmware watches its analog channel while it sleeps,
unconditionally — src/ulp_watch.cpp is compiled into every image the generator emits, because
the generator reads the ssn:implements this class entails, which makes the class's claim
self-keeping. It lands on the CONNECTING device alone, which is exactly the per-channel truth:
the typed device IS the analog channel, and the DHT channels riding the same stream are never
typed, so they promise nothing — honest by absence, with no one having to say so per world.
What holds the promise to the silicon is the wiring shape (mc:WatchedChannelWiringShape):
a board's class states which pin roles its sleep-watcher reaches, and a watched channel whose
signal pin the watcher cannot reach refuses to validate.

Simulated devices run no firmware and never carry this type: sim:simulatedBy is their
kind-statement, and a stand-in typed with a firmware class it does not run would be a new way
to lie.""" .

:Node a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "governed node" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf device:Device ,
        #  The two facts every board of this kind shares, reaching each instance through the
        #  closure's hasValue rule — a datasheet fact, except the sheet is src/main.cpp:
        #  the agent commands the cadence and the board keeps it (Scheduled), and the
        #  generator finds this directory by the name the class states once.
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sensing:senseMode ;
          owl:hasValue sensing:ScheduledProcedure ] ,
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mc:firmware ;
          owl:hasValue "moisture-sensor" ] ,
        #  The alarm promise (#181), on the class because it is true of every flashed image:
        #  the ULP watches the commanded band and delta while everything sleeps
        #  (src/ulp_watch.cpp), and the generator emits WAKE_ON_ALARM from exactly this
        #  entailment. Only the connecting device is typed, so the promise reaches the analog
        #  channel and never the DHT's — per channel by construction, not by statement.
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ssn:implements ;
          owl:hasValue sensing:AlarmProcedure ] ;
    rdfs:comment "A board the agent governs: it takes the cadence, the verdict and the alarm limits from the retained command, keeps the interval while deep-sleeping, acks the cadence each reading (#135), and waits to be released after publishing (#152). One clock, the agent's — the mode above is not an opinion but what src/main.cpp does." .
